        "step_number": 1,
        "text": "Step 1: Use the change of base formula for logarithms, which states that $\\log_a{b} = \\frac{\\log_c{b}}{\\log_c{a}}$ for any positive $a$, $b$, and $c$ where $a \\neq 1$ and $c \\neq 1$. Applying this to each term in the product:\n\n\\[\n\\log_2{3} \\cdot \\log_3{4} \\cdot \\log_4{5} \\cdot \\log_5{6} \\cdot \\log_6{7} \\cdot \\log_7{8} = \\left(\\frac{\\log{3}}{\\log{2}}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\log{4}}{\\log{3}}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\log{5}}{\\log{4}}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\log{6}}{\\log{5}}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\log{7}}{\\log{6}}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\log{8}}{\\log{7}}\\right)\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 990,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 991,
        "char_start": 578,
        "effective_step": 2,
        "end_token_index": 393,
        "near_end": false,
        "prefix_token_count": 394,
        "step_number": 2,
        "text": "Step 2: Notice that this is a telescoping product, where most terms will cancel out. Specifically, all the numerators (except the first one) and all the denominators (except the last one) will cancel out:\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{\\log{3}}{\\log{2}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log{4}}{\\log{3}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log{5}}{\\log{4}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log{6}}{\\log{5}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log{7}}{\\log{6}} \\cdot \\frac{\\log{8}}{\\log{7}} = \\frac{\\log{8}}{\\log{2}}\n\\]"
      },
      {
        "aligned_char_end": 1155,
        "boundary_aligned_back": true,
        "char_end": 1156,
        "char_start": 993,
        "effective_step": 3,
        "end_token_index": 471,
        "near_end": true,
        "prefix_token_count": 472,
        "step_number": 3,
        "text": "Step 3: Simplify the remaining fraction. Since $8 = 2^3$, we have $\\log{8} = \\log{(2^3)} = 3\\log{2}$:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log{8}}{\\log{2}} = \\frac{3\\log{2}}{\\log{2}} = 3\n\\]"
      }
